Ladybird adjusts the text of an SVG <text> element to its textLength,
by adding space after each typographic character but the last,
or by scaling the text along the inline axis where lengthAdjust
is spacingAndGlyphs. Blink does that in SvgTextLayoutAlgorithm::
ResolveTextLength(), Gecko in SVGTextFrame::DoGlyphPositioning(), and
WebKit in SVGTextLayoutAlgorithm::applyTextLength().

For a textLength on a <text>, the new test gives the same character
positions in Chrome 157.0.8103.79 and Firefox 151.0 for every case but
the percentage and those with a dx: Blink resolves a percentage against
the viewport’s width, as Ladybird does, and Gecko and WebKit against its
diagonal. WebKit 26.5 agrees on the spacing cases, but reports unscaled
positions under spacingAndGlyphs. A length of zero is ignored in Blink,
WebKit, and Ladybird, and applied in Gecko.

A dx on a character after the first counts toward the length in
Ladybird, which is what SVG 2 says, and what Blink does: Chrome scales
such a dx under spacingAndGlyphs, and takes it out of the space it adds
otherwise. Gecko adjusts the advances alone, and keeps the dx as it is.
WebKit 26.5 agrees with Chrome where the dx comes from a list on the
<text> itself, but places the characters differently from both where a
child element has the dx.

The engines disagree on a textLength on a <tspan>: Blink adjusts the
<tspan>’s text and shifts what follows, WebKit adjusts it without
shifting what follows, and Gecko applies only the outer <text>’s
attributes. Ladybird ignores it, as Gecko does.

Under spacingAndGlyphs, Blink and WebKit scale a stroke along with the
glyphs, since they scale at paint time. Ladybird scales the outlines and
then strokes them, as Gecko does. Where space is added, Ladybird shapes
the text without optional ligatures, as it does for letter-spacing,
where Blink keeps the ligatures it shaped.
